[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: [1mhow the shared module gets loaded by both sides.[0m This one's structural and awkward, because the two halves use
  incompatible module systems today — [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS ([38;5;153mrequire[39m/[38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m), while [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a classic [38;5;153m<script>[39m with
  no module system at all. One file can't be consumed by both as-is.

  - [1mESM everywhere[0m — logger is an ES module; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m gets [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m,
    and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m + [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m convert to [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m. Cleanest result and no wrapper boilerplate, but it edits
    two files that have nothing to do with logging, and [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m means [38;5;153mapp.js[39m no longer runs from a [38;5;153mfile://[39m open —
    it needs to be served.
  - [1mUMD-style wrapper[0m — one file that detects [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m and otherwise attaches to [38;5;153mwindow[39m. Nothing else in the repo
    changes and [38;5;153mfile://[39m keeps working; the cost is a few lines of dated boilerplate at the top of the logger and a
    global.
  - [1mShared core + two thin adapters[0m — core logic in one file, a CJS adapter and a browser adapter. Most files, cleanest
    separation; overkill at this size.

  I recommend the UMD wrapper. It's the only option that touches zero unrelated files, and your CLAUDE.md is explicit
  about minimal, focused changes. The boilerplate is ugly but it's five lines, and it's reversible — if you later move
  the project to ESM wholesale, the logger converts with it.
